<section id="sticky">

  <header>
    <h2>Sticky</h2>
  </header>

	<p><strong>Sticky notifications</strong> remain on screen until explicitly dismissed, useful for critical alerts or messages that require acknowledgment. Anchor them near related content or at the top of a container, and avoid covering essential controls.</p>

	<div class="section-example">
		<div class="container browser">
			<div class="notification sticky yellow">
				<p>The catalogue will be <strong>unavailable on Sunday between 02:00 and 04:00</strong> for scheduled maintenance.</p>
				<a class="control close">
					<svg>
						<use xlink:href="/images/icons.svg#clear"></use>
					</svg>
				</a>
			</div>
			<main>
				<p >Sticky notifications stay at the top of their container while the content below scrolls underneath them.</p>
				<p >That makes them suitable for messages that should remain visible, such as planned maintenance or an unsaved draft.</p>
				<blockquote >A sticky message is only useful when it is important. Too many of them and people stop reading.</blockquote>
				<p >Always include a way to close the notification, and remember the choice so it doesn’t come back on the next page.</p>
			</main>
		</div>
	</div>
  
  <div class="code-example">
      
    <pre><code class="html">&lt;div class="notification sticky"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>

  </div>
</section>
